# Build output
/target
**/target

# ui-verify run output. A screenshot or a diag trace describes ONE run of
# ONE build; it is not source and it does not survive the next run's meaning.
/tools/ui-verify/out
*.diag.txt

# ★★★ A sweep's output directory, under the same rule one level out: a TRACE
# is evidence for a single run, a SUMMARY is a record. `sweep-*/‹family›.txt`
# — a few hundred lines naming what passed — may be committed; the per-check
# traces and PNGs behind it may not. A single sweep produces hundreds of
# megabytes across hundreds of files, the operator's disk is tight, and
# `target/` already grows unbounded without a second unpruned pile beside it.
/evidence/sweep-*/*/
/evidence/sweep-*/*.png

# ★★ `evidence/` ITSELF IS NOT IGNORED, and that is deliberate. It holds the
# pixel oracle's calibration input `crop_settings.png`, which `ui-verify`
# READS: `profile.rs`'s `pdfcer-legacy` region set is expressed as fractions
# of that image, and `tests/pixel_oracle_against_real_evidence.rs` SKIPs —
# green, silently — when it is absent. Anything else that lands in this
# directory is a tool's output; only an input a check reads belongs in git.

# OS / editor
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store
.tmpwork/

# Cygwin/MSYS crash dumps, produced by the shell rather than by this project.
*.stackdump

# Python bytecode from the gate instruments. Importing `verb-coverage.py` to
# spot-check its matcher on 2026-09-05 left a `tools/__pycache__/` behind that
# `git status` then reported as untracked source.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# ★ Acrobat's extracted resource strings — `tools/acrobat-form-strings.py`'s
# output. Deliberately NOT committed even though `evidence/` generally is, and
# not because of size: `strings.txt` is ~750 KB of Adobe's own UTF-16LE
# resource content lifted verbatim out of `AcroForm.api` and `AcrobatRes.dll`,
# and THIS REPOSITORY IS PUBLIC. Quoting the few dozen control labels that a
# parity table has to name is ordinary interoperability documentation;
# republishing an entire product's string table is not.
#
# Regenerate before following any `ACRO:` citation in `FORMS_PARITY.md`:
#     python tools/acrobat-form-strings.py
# Line numbers and offsets in those citations are Acrobat-version-specific —
# `FORMS_PARITY.md` §10 records the version they were taken against, and the
# tool writes the version it found into its own output header. A different
# Acrobat will produce the same labels at different coordinates.
evidence/acrobat-forms/*.txt
